  "data": {
    "title": "Visual search task immediate training effects on task-related functional connectivity",
    "text": "Title: Visual search task immediate training effects on task-related functional connectivity\n\nPost-processing analysis: individual local and distant degree maps\nAt the individual level, a whole-brain voxel-wise Pearson correlation coefficient between each voxel pair’s time course was estimated for each participant and session. A mask of 6332 voxels was used that covered the entire brain. Only positive correlations were retained, and negative r-values were removed due to their controversial interpretation in functional connectivity MRI.\n\nDegree analyses were used to study task-related functional connectivity. Degree analyses make it possible to differentiate between regional connections (local links) and interregional connections (distant links) based on physical distance between voxels. The optimal neighborhood threshold was set to 21 mm to segregate local (<21 mm radius) and distant (>21 mm radius) connectivity.\n\nTo classify results, the 7-network estimate parcellation of the human cerebral cortex (visual, sensorimotor, dorsal attention, ventral attention, limbic, frontoparietal control, and default mode networks) was applied to the degree maps. Network overlap analysis was conducted using in-house MATLAB code.\n\nGraph-based measures focused on degree centrality, quantifying the number of local and distant links departing from each voxel. Group-level effects were assessed using a 2×2 ANOVA comparing training and control groups across pre-training and post-training sessions."
  },
